==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|way out of a wet paper bag}}

===Etymology===
Most likely originating as, ''He couldn't [[punch]] his way out of a [[paper bag]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-way out of a paper bag.ogg|a=AU}}

===Phrase===
{{head|en|phrase|head=[[way]] [[out of]] [[a]] [[paper bag]]}}

# {{lb|en|slang|idiomatic|formulaic}} A [[minimal]] [[level]] of [[competence]] or [[effectiveness]], as used in [[phrase]]s where one is unable to [[perform]].
#: {{ux|en|My boss is so clueless, he couldn't schedule his own '''way out of a paper bag'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Junior varsity couldn't play their '''way out of a paper bag''' in comparison.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1923|title=Asia|volume=23|publisher=American Asiatic Association|page=281
|passage=The old chap did not look as though he could push his '''way out of a paper bag'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1924|author=Harry Charles Witwer|title=Love and Learn|publisher=G. P. Putnam's sons|page=260
|passage=Personally I don’t think this egg could punch his '''way out of a paper bag'''. He’s only been workin’ in this trap two days<!--,-->}}
#* {{RQ:Truss Eats, Shoots & Leaves|page=8|passage=To be fair, many people who couldn’t punctuate their '''way out of a paper bag''' are still interested in the way punctuation can alter the sense of a string of words. It is the basis of all “I’m sorry, I’ll read that again” jokes.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Allie Peter|title=Saved by the Fireman|page=105
|passage=“You'd hate his cooking[,” he said.] </br> “Most guys can't cook  their '''way out of a paper bag'''[,” Charlotte said.]}}

